'''''Spiraea salicifolia''''', the '''bridewort''', '''willow-leaved meadowsweet''', '''spice hardhack''', or '''Aaron's beard''', is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Rosaceae]].<ref name="RHS_17690" >{{cite web |title=''Spiraea salicifolia'' bridewort [3] |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/17690/i-spiraea-salicifolia-i/details |date=2023 |publisher=The Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=6 February 2023 |quote=Other common names; spice hardhack, willow-leaved meadowsweet, Aaron's beard [3] ... 1 suppliers }}</ref> A shrub, it is native to east-central Europe, Kazakhstan, all of Siberia, the Russian Far East, Mongolia, northern China, Korea, and Japan, and it has been widely introduced to the rest of Europe and to eastern North America.<ref name="POWO_30200186-2" >{{cite POWO |id=30200186-2 |title=''Spiraea salicifolia'' L. |access-date=6 February 2023 }}</ref> It has been cultivated since the 1500s for hedges and similar applications, but is not particularly well-behaved.<ref name="treesandshrubsonline" >{{cite web |title=''Spiraea salicifolia'' L. |url=https://treesandshrubsonline.org/articles/spiraea/spiraea-salicifolia |website=Trees and Shrubs Online |publisher=International Dendrology Society |access-date=6 February 2023 }}</ref>

==Subtaxa==
The following varieties are accepted:<ref name="POWO_30200186-2" />
*''Spiraea salicifolia'' var. ''grosseserrata'' {{small|Liou & Liou f.}} – Manchuria
*''Spiraea salicifolia'' var. ''salicifolia'' – Entire range

